entry radon
term: Radon
related_terms: background-radiation
related_terms: decay
related_terms: alpha-particle
related_terms: exposure
quick_facts: Noble gas from uranium decay in the ground
quick_facts: #2 cause of lung cancer overall, #1 among non-smokers
quick_facts: Pool in basements — heavier than air
quick_facts: WHO action reference: ~300 Bq/m³ indoors
quick_facts: Fixed cheaply with sub-slab depressurization vents

sources:
    {"organization":"EPA","title":"Radon","url":"https://www.epa.gov/radon"}
    {"organization":"WHO","title":"Radon and Health","url":"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/radon-and-health"}

definition:
    A colorless, odorless radioactive gas seeping from trace uranium in soil, rock, and building materials — the heaviest gas and a noble one, so it drifts freely into basements and lower floors. Its short-lived daughter products stick in lungs and emit alpha particles there, making radon the leading cause of lung cancer among non-smokers and the single largest natural dose for most people. Cheap test kits and sub-slab ventilation fix problem buildings.

debunking_snippet:
    “Radon is only a problem in uranium mines.” It's everywhere — the average home holds some, and tightly sealed modern houses can concentrate it. Testing costs less than a pizza.
